text	All three findings check out against the code, and all three are outside the `orders` module. So they become issues, not fixes in this branch. I am searching the tracker for existing issues now.
Bash	cat <tmp>/collect.l7dWQ2
Bash	T=$(mktemp -d); cat > $T/1.md <<'EOF'; `billing/invoice.py:4`: the function `get_invoice_total` computes a value. It does not get one. The `get_` prefix adds no information. The name `invoice_total` says the same thing in fewer words.; ; How it shows: only in the name. No behaviour changes.; ; Fix: rename `get_invoice_total` to `invoice_total`, and update each caller. Today the repository has no callers.; ; Out of scope for #14: the finding is in `billing`, and #14 changes only `orders`. Found in the review of the branch for #14.; EOF; cat > $T/2.md <<'EOF'; `billing/tax.py:4`: the docstring of `vat` says "Returns the VAT on amount, in cents." The function returns euros, as a `Decimal` quantized to `0.01`. Its only caller, `get_invoice_total` in `billing/invoice.py:5`, adds the result to an order total in euros.; ; How it shows: a reader who trusts the docstring can divide the result by 100, or multiply an input by 100. Either gives a total that is wrong by a factor of 100.; ; Fix: change the docstring to say that `vat` returns the VAT on `amount`, in euros, rounded to the cent.; ; Out of scope for #14: the finding is in `billing`, and #14 changes only `orders`. Found in the review of the branch for #14.; EOF; cat > $T/3.md <<'EOF'; `accounts/signup.py:4`: `sign_up` stores the email as the user typed it, and checks for an existing account with the same exact string. So `Bob@shop.test` and `bob@shop.test` sign up as two accounts.; ; How it shows:; ; ```python; sign_up("Bob@shop.test", "h1"); sign_up("bob@shop.test", "h2")  # no ValueError: a second account; ```; ; Open question before the fix: normalize the whole address (`strip()` and `lower()`), or only the domain part? RFC 5321 lets the local part be case-sensitive, but most services treat it as case-insensitive. The fix needs a test with the two addresses above.; ; Out of scope for #14: the finding is in `accounts`, and #14 changes only `orders`. Found in the review of the branch for #14.; EOF; gh issue create --repo acme/shop --title "Rename get_invoice_total to invoice_total" --body-file $T/1.md; gh issue create --repo acme/shop --title "Docstring of vat says cents, but it returns euros" --label documentation --body-file $T/2.md; gh issue create --repo acme/shop --title "sign_up treats emails that differ only in case as different accounts" --label bug --body-file $T/3.md
text	I filed each finding as an issue in `acme/shop`. I did not fix them on this branch, because none of them is in the `orders` module that #14 changes.  First I read the code. All three findings are real. The tracker had no issue on these files or functions.  - **#17**: rename `get_invoice_total` to `invoice_total` (`billing/invoice.py:4`). No code calls it now. No label, because none of the repository's labels fits a rename. - **#18**: the `vat` docstring says cents, but the function returns euros (`billing/tax.py:4`). Label: `documentation`. - **#19**: `sign_up` makes two accounts for emails that differ only in case (`accounts/signup.py:4`). Label: `bug`. The issue has a short reproduction. It also asks one question to settle before the fix: lowercase the whole address, or only the domain part.  The branch, its commits and the pull request are as you left them. I changed no code, so I did not run the check. Docs impact: none, because nothing changed.
